DRIFT="No feature set comes close to 0.5, so the test years are clearly recognisable and all models have to extrapolate to some degree. Dropping the absolute price columns lowers the AUC from 0.953 to 0.921, and the LP columns alone give 0.869, the lowest here, which fits the LP being mostly about relative economics. My first version of this check included calendar columns and gave results in the wrong order (a larger set scoring below its own subset), which is what led me to the memorisation explanation above. So I removed them. Drift alone did not decide anything: the absolute columns stayed in the net because validation favoured them (Section 5.3)."
PERM="Both parts of the hypothesis held. Shuffling the LP schedule group costs about 0.24 in both models (0.22 in the blend), far more than any other group, followed by LP sensitivity (about 0.02). Market columns cost the net 0.0087 against 0.0005 for the trees. Calendar and water state matter little in both once the LP is present. One result I did not predict: the net loses 0.082 for the unit group and 0.065 for `unit_index` alone, against 0.004 and 0.002 for the trees. The net also has a learned unit embedding, so I expected it not to need this column, and I don't know why it relies on it this much. The blend is much less sensitive to it (0.0018), which is one concrete benefit of blending. Single features cost little, at most 0.002 in the blend, because the LP columns are strongly related and can replace each other. So these bars understate the features and should only be read together with the group table. They are from one shuffle and one seed, and differences below about 0.0002 aren't meaningful (`lp_week_u` even gives a gain of 0.0002 when shuffled)."
ERROR="- **By unit.** Learning adds most where the LP is weakest: Vinje G3 goes from 0.929 to 0.976, Hogga from 0.938 to 0.982, Kjela from 0.950 to 0.989. Those three remain the weakest. Byrte (0.9995) and the Tokke units (about 0.996) are nearly solved. The net and the trees are close on every unit, and the blend matches or beats both on all 14. The net's logits correlate 0.97 with the trees', so the gain from blending is modest.\n# - **By unit and year.** The weakest cells are Lio in 2022 (0.964), Vinje G3 in 2020 (0.964), Hogga in 2017 (0.968) and Haukeli in 2022 (0.974). The weakest year is 2018 (0.99037). In phase 1, 2020 was by far the weakest (0.97653 for E021a). It's now 0.99214, so the LP removed most of the low-price problem.\n# - **By price and month.** Performance is nearly flat across price octiles (0.992-0.996), which supports the blend not depending much on regime in training years. By month, April is weakest for both the blend and the LP (0.988 and 0.958), with July next. I did not investigate why.\n# - **Level and timing.** Knowing the true weekly on-share and nothing else gives 0.981. The blend's own weekly level alone gives 0.973, so there is still more to gain in the level, as in phase 1.\n# - **Calibration.** Close to the diagonal, with slight overconfidence at low probabilities (predicted 0.05, observed about 0.11). The metric only uses the ranking, so I did not calibrate further."
